<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Borama is a city in the Awdal region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, inhabited mainly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadabuursi">Gadabuursi</a> clan. In 1993 it hosted the Borama conference of clan elders, which adopted a national charter for Somaliland and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haji-ibrahim-egal">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal</a> as president. It is the main town of Somaliland's western highlands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borama.jpg" alt="Borama" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mashhuur · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borama_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Borama lies in a mountainous and hilly area near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> word booraan, a deep hole or geological depression such as a basin or valley, so that Borama means place of valleys. It has green meadows and fields, unusually fertile in a largely arid countryside, and these have drawn gazelles, birds and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>. Its climate is hot semi-arid, with June the hottest month at an average of 24.1 °C and January the coolest at 17.1. The Awdal region around it is inhabited mainly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadabuursi">Gadabuursi</a> clan, a subclan of the Dir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds in the Borama area, as around nearby Amud, include the remains of tombs, houses and mosques and sherds of Oriental wares, particularly Chinese porcelain. They range from the 12th to the 18th centuries, with most from the 15th and 16th, a time of great commercial activity associated with the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, which ruled the area. Excavations at more than fourteen sites near the town in the late 1800s and early 1900s unearthed coins from the time of Kait Bey, the eighteenth Burji Mamluk sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and most finds were sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><p>In the first half of the 20th century Borama formed part of the British Somaliland protectorate. The modern town was founded in 1921 by Ughaz Elmi Warfa, ughaz of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadabuursi">Gadabuursi</a>, at the direction of the British, and it became a district in 1925. In 1933 Sheikh Abdurahman Sheikh Nuur, a Qur'anic teacher and son of the town's qadi, devised a new orthography for writing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a>.</p><p>The Borama or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadabuursi">Gadabuursi</a> script was used mainly by Nuur, his circle and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> and Borama merchants. After independence Borama was administered as part of the Awdal region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, and in the Ogaden War of the late 1970s Ethiopian forces aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-national-movement">Somali National Movement</a> bombed it from the air along with several other northern cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Borama is the largest city of the Awdal region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> and its commercial seat. A 2023 estimate gave the city about 300,000 people. It was the first area in Somaliland to adopt a self-help scheme after the civil war. It has an airport named for Aden Isaq Ahmed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>'s first Minister of Education, and 52 primary and secondary schools. Notable people from the city include Hassan Sheikh Mumin, a poet, playwright and composer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Borama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The SNM chairman Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur became the first president, and a second conference at <b>Borama</b> in 1993 transferred power to Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal, a former prime minister of the Somali Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
